      "uuid": "5e2fd58e-5d9f-4fc8-ad6f-f6e37afefb73",
      "canonical_url": "https://mathdb.com/p/379844/conjecture-on-nonseparating-cycle-types-in-nonorientable-tri",
      "title": "Conjecture on nonseparating cycle types in nonorientable triangulations",
      "statement": "Let $N_g$ denote the nonorientable surface of Euler genus $g$. A nonseparating cycle is **one-sided** when its neighborhood is a M\u00f6bius band, **two-sided** otherwise; it is **orientable-leaving** when deleting it leaves an orientable surface, and **nonorientable-leaving** otherwise.\n\n**Conjecture on nonseparating cycle types.** If $g \\geq 3$ is odd, every triangulation of $N_g$ has a nonseparating cycle that is one-sided and orientable-leaving, one that is one-sided and nonorientable-leaving, and one that is two-sided and nonorientable-leaving. If $g \\geq 4$ is even, every triangulation of $N_g$ has a nonseparating cycle that is one-sided and nonorientable-leaving, one that is two-sided and orientable-leaving, and one that is two-sided and nonorientable-leaving.\n\nThe assertion extends the verified low-genus pattern for $N_1$, $N_2$, $N_3$, and $N_4$. No proof or counterexample is supplied for the stated general ranges.",
      "statement_completeness": "full",
      "post_type": "conjecture",
